  "description": "Claude Code–style workflow kit for macOS, Linux and Windows, installed whole or as the modules you choose: objective answers (an objection is a reason to check again, not to give in; the user's guess at a cause is one hypothesis; «как думаешь?» gets a verdict, not praise; agreement without a look at anything sends the agent back), facts, not guesses (the agent's final report is held against the conversation — test results and their numbers, CI status, deploys, pushes, checks, files, line numbers, links, versions and prices — questions get looked up with a source for each fact, and fake checks are refused), parallel work (the agent hands independent parts of a task to subagents — explorer, researcher, worker, test-writer, reviewers — in one step and keeps working instead of waiting or polling; /parallel), speed and context (commands that failed in the terminal sandbox are remembered per project and run outside it from the start, a context budget with targeted reads, a stop to loops of failing commands), an agent that sets up projects on its own (creates a git repository with a safe first commit, writes AGENTS.md with /init, records project rules) and changes settings for you (/settings: MCP servers, plugin options) while its own permissions stay yours to change, several agents on one project without overwriting each other, full access on a single grant (the agent runs every command itself), Bypass Mode switched on once with /bypass and remembered for every conversation (no permission prompts; only system-level actions — sudo, system folders, disks, autostart, keys, the agent's own permissions — still ask), Git and GitHub (/git: the repository's state in one command, branches and syncing, conflicts step by step, history — who changed what and why, safe undo, GitHub issues, pull requests, review threads and releases through gh; git and gh commands that would hang the agent are refused with the form that works, and no rebase or merge is left half done), code navigation through language servers (/lsp: the agent asks pyright, sourcekit-lsp, kotlin-lsp, typescript-language-server, gopls, rust-analyzer or clangd — not rg — where a symbol is defined, every place it is used with the function each is in, who calls it and what it calls, its type, a file's outline; type errors an edit added; a rename across files; servers stay warm in the background), /ci (tracks GitHub Actions and GitLab CI runs in the background after git push or /pr without polling loops, extracts cleaned failure logs, checks CI before /deploy, and holds «CI is green» claims to facts), /deploy with checks and rollback, /ui-check (headless Chrome screenshots), /mobile (the agent runs the app in the iOS simulator or the Android emulator and uses it itself: installs and launches a build, looks at screenshots, lists what is on the screen, taps by label, swipes, types, reads the app's errors and crashes, checks dark mode and large text — and isn't done with a changed screen until it has looked at it), /standup, /hooks, secret protection, auto-memory shared with Claude Code where the agent works out on its own when and what to save — a correction or a standing preference, how the servers and deploys work, the cause of a stubborn failure — with no secrets let in, and with each request the saved memories it is about (/memory, /remember), /handoff to continue work in a new conversation (recent conversations shown at start), a Stop hook that sends the agent back to unfinished task.md items, automatic checks of every edited file (syntax and the project's linters), commands the agent runs on its own (/review after big changes, /security-review, /handoff), instructions that survive long conversations and context compaction, project rules (/init, CLAUDE.md compatibility with @imports and .claude/rules), git workflow (/commit, /pr, /review, /security-review, /simplify, /fix-issue), /usage cost dashboard plus a live side panel with tokens, context fill and cost, /export, /doctor, /update (once a day the agent says a new release is out and what it brings; one command installs it — a fast-forward of your clone or a download — with a rollback; auto-update optional), subagents (explorer, researcher, worker, code-reviewer, security-auditor, debugger, test-writer), a git-context session hook and a guard that asks before destructive commands and before pushing when you said not to.",
